A physical QED amplitude with one external photon of momentum k and polarization ε may be written iℳ=ε_μ ℳ^μ. The Ward identity is

$$ k_μ ℳ^μ(k) = 0

so that ℳ is unchanged if ε_μ is shifted by any multiple of k_μ, which is a residual gauge transformation of the polarization.

### Worked example 1

Why is this the same statement as "a longitudinal photon does not scatter"? A polarization ε_μ∝k_μ is a pure gauge (in momentum space); the identity says the amplitude for emitting or absorbing that mode is zero.

# 2 Tree-level proof, one charged line

## 2.1 Emission from an external electron

Suppose the photon attaches to an outgoing electron of momentum p′, so the vertex is ū(p′)(ieγ^μ) and p′=p+k. Replacing ε_μ by k_μ gives ū(p′)γ^μ k_μ=ū(p′)k̸. But k̸=p̸′−p̸, so

$$ ū(p′) k̸ = ū(p′)(p̸′ − p̸) = ū(p′)(m − p̸)

using ū(p′)(p̸′−m)=0. The remaining ū(p′)(m−p̸) then cancels against the adjacent electron propagator or the incoming spinor by the same Dirac equation, leaving zero (Exercise 6 works the incoming-photon-on-incoming-electron case the same way).

## 2.2 Attachment along an internal line

If the photon can attach at more than one point along a charged line, replacing ε by k produces a telescoping difference of inverse propagators, S⁻¹(p+k)−S⁻¹(p), which is exactly the Ward identity in the form

$$ k_μ Γ^μ(p+k, p) = S⁻¹(p+k) − S⁻¹(p)

quoted in this course's formula index. Summing every attachment point along the line, the interior terms cancel and the ends are killed by on-shell spinors, so the full amplitude still vanishes.

### Worked example 2

State why a diagram with the photon attached to a closed fermion loop, and no other charged external legs, already vanishes when contracted with k_μ. The loop is a closed charged line with no on-shell ends; the telescoping sum around a loop is identically zero, which is Lesson 62's transversality.

# 3 Consequences this course has been using

## 3.1 Polarization sums

The physical sum is over two transverse polarizations, Σε_μ ε_ν=−g_μν+(gauge terms built from k). Those extra terms contract with ℳ^μℳ^ν and vanish by Section 1, so

$$ Σ |ε_μ ℳ^μ|² = (− g_(μν)) ℳ^μ ℳ^ν

which is Lesson 51's covariant replacement.

## 3.2 The photon propagator

Lesson 70's ξ-dependent piece is proportional to k_μk_ν. Inserted between two conserved vertices, it vanishes by the same identity, so Feynman gauge ξ=1 may be used in any physical |ℳ|². This completes Lesson 47.

## 3.3 Vacuum polarization

q_μ Π^μν(q)=0 follows by applying Section 2.2 to the closed loop of Lesson 62. Combined with Lorentz covariance, Π^μν=(q²g^μν−q^μq^ν)Π(q²), as Lesson 62 previewed and Lesson 66 can check after regularization.

# 4 Loops and infrared photons

The same telescoping works diagram by diagram at one loop provided a gauge-invariant regulator is used. A hard cutoff can break the identity; that is one reason Lesson 66's cutoff was a teaching device, and why a fully consistent treatment of loops uses a regulator that preserves k_μℳ^μ=0. Infrared photons, Lesson 73, require the identity in a different way: soft emission amplitudes are themselves proportional to e p·ε/p·k, which is gauge invariant because of this same contraction.

