# Solutions

**1.** Contracting the photon's momentum into the amplitude, with the polarization stripped off, gives zero.

**2.** Δℳ=λ k_μ ℳ^μ=0.

**3.** ū(p′)k̸=ū(p′)(p̸′−p̸)=ū(p′)(m−p̸), which cancels against the rest of the line.

**4.** (p̸−m)u(p)=0 similarly kills the incoming end.

**5.** Attachment at p versus p+k produces S(p+k) k̸ S(p)=S(p)−S(p+k) after using k̸=S⁻¹(p+k)−S⁻¹(p), a difference that telescopes.

**6.** Incoming photon k absorbed by incoming electron p, outgoing p′=p+k: u-bar side as in Section 2.1, or the incoming spinor (p̸−m)u=0 after k̸=p̸′−p̸.

**7.** S⁻¹(p+k)−S⁻¹(p)=(p̸+k̸−m)−(p̸−m)=k̸=k_μ γ^μ=k_μ Γ^μ_tree.

**8.** Immediate from Exercise 7.

**9.** The difference between the transverse projector and −g_μν is built from k; it vanishes against ℳ^μℳ^ν.

**10.** The (1−ξ)k_μk_ν/k² piece.

**11.** Summing attachments around a closed loop telescopes to zero with no leftover on-shell ends.

**12.** Cutting |k_E|<Λ is not a gauge-invariant restriction; it can generate a spurious longitudinal piece.

**13.** Residual χ with □χ=0 are plane-wave gauges, i.e. ε∝k; the Ward identity says those modes drop from amplitudes.

**14.** Each diagram separately is not gauge invariant; their sum is. Dropping one would leave a residual k_μ ℳ^μ.

**15.** k_{1μ} ℳ^{μν}=0 and k_{2ν} ℳ^{μν}=0 separately, for on-shell photons.

**16.** k·p′/p′·k − k·p/p·k=1−1=0.

